  "testbed_rationale": "This paper is a high-level community assessment and living-data synthesis of global carbon sinks and sources, utilizing multi-model ensembles and observation products rather than acting as a benchmark dataset for training ML climate parameterizations.",
  "pain_points": [
    "Divergence in multi-decadal trends between observation-based surface ocean fCO2 products and ocean biogeochemistry models (GOBMs), particularly in data-sparse regions like the Southern Ocean.",
    "Persisting large uncertainty in land-use change emissions (ELUC) and lack of consistency across bookkeeping models and dynamic global vegetation models.",
    "Low agreement between atmospheric inversion models and dynamic global vegetation models on the partitioning of northern extra-tropical vs. tropical land sinks.",
    "Data sparsity in surface ocean carbon observations, which continues to decline back to early-2000s levels."
  ],
  "extraction_notes": "Machine learning (specifically feed-forward neural networks) is utilized within the paper primarily for projecting the current year (2024) ocean sink SOCEAN and terrestrial sink SLAND based on historical data and ENSO indicators. Additionally, ML (SOM-FFN, etc.) is employed by several underlying fCO2 data products (e.g., VLIZ-SOMFFN, CMEMS-LSCE-FFNNv2) that are synthesized in this budget.",
